  "notes": "The calf is never drawn; no gold or idol motif. Light only for their plea for mercy. Musa: no motif. STAGING v4: The camp of the Children of Israel in the desert after the sea (desert setting; the sources do not name the place: generic wilderness). The calf is never drawn, and nothing gold (no coins or jewel plate). The people who took the calf are three faceless men (a group doing a blameworthy act: no woman is singled out). c1 (148): Musa is away (not drawn) and the camera is wide when he is named (148:3); an hourglass for his absence (148:4-6, Al-Muyassar: after he left for the appointment); the people are not shown worshipping or bowing: on 148:20 (they took it) they turn their backs. c2 (149): Musa (blue drop) comes in on 149:1 (both tafsirs: their regret came when Musa returned); they wring their hands in regret (149:2), a two-paths plate for they saw they had gone astray (149:5-8), and they raise their hands in their plea for mercy (149:9, 149:14); no plate stands over their du'a, no bowing."
